<Doc>
    <p>The pipeline lives at <code>src/pipeline</code>: deals in four stages, a forecast, a search,
        and a few panels that all read the same deals. Each card below is one of those panels, with
        the handler it calls and the test the app runs on it.</p>

    <Section title="A call with the same arguments is one load">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-stage-shared"
            app="crm-04"
            title={'`getStage({ stage })` — one entry per set of arguments'}
            summary="Three panels, each with its own stage picker. Put one on won, and then the others."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageTiles.abide': stageTilesSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageCount.abide': stageCountSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageValue.abide': stageValueSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageAverage.abide': stageAverageSource,
                'src/pipeline/getStage.handler.ts': getStageSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageTiles.test.ts': stageTilesTestSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageTiles.bench.ts': stageTilesBenchSource,
            }}
        >
            <StageTiles />
        </Example>

        <p>The bench puts all three panels on one stage once each entry has been answered, among 100
            deals and among 1,000. Its ratio is what a pick costs as the pipeline grows: the panels
            read the entry the server summed, and never the deals.</p>

        <p>The three panels never speak to each other, and nothing is lifted into a parent. When
            they ask for the same stage they get the same entry, so the later panels paint at once,
            with no request. On two stages they are two entries, and each
            pays for its own.</p>

        <p>A body with a parameter is a keyed value: one entry per set of arguments, held. That is
            true of a handler like <code>getStage</code>, and of <code>{'reactive((args) => …)'}</code>
            written in a page. The arguments are its <code>Args</code>, and the key is their JSON with
            the properties sorted, so argument order never splits an entry.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code> body, keyed</td><td><code>{'reactive(({ stage }) => …)'}</code> holds one entry per stage. Its body does not track what it reads; triggers reload it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>k.busy</code>, <code>k.ready</code></td><td>whether any entry of a keyed value, or those a partial <code>Args</code> matches, is loading or has landed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.busy</code>, <code>rpc.ready</code></td><td>the same two on a handler: <code>{"getStage.busy({ stage: 'won' })"}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>global</code></td><td><code>{'{ global: true }'}</code> shares one cache across every caller in the process, where the default is one per request on a server and one per tab in a browser</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`refresh` reloads now, and `invalidate` marks stale">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-stale"
            app="crm-04"
            title="`refresh()` and `invalidate()` — the two ways back to the server"
            summary="The weighted forecast, with both buttons under it. Press Reload now, then Mark stale."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/Forecast.abide': forecastSource,
                'src/pipeline/getForecast.handler.ts': getForecastSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/Forecast.test.ts': forecastTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Forecast />
        </Example>

        <p><code>refresh()</code> loads now and keeps the forecast on screen while it does: the
            value is <code>busy()</code> and still <code>ready()</code>, and it swaps when the new one
            lands. <code>invalidate()</code> loads nothing. It marks what is held as wrong, and the
            next read fetches.</p>

        <p>Reach for one or the other, not both. A reload over a value marked stale has nothing
            trusted to serve, so the figure blanks until it lands.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>s.refresh</code></td><td>reloads one value now, serving what is held until the new one lands</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.invalidate</code></td><td>marks one value stale; the next read loads</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ttl</code></td><td><code>{'{ ttl: 60_000 }'}</code> lets a value expire a minute after it loaded. Expiry wakes nobody, and the next read loads. On a value with nothing to load from, it is how long a write stands: see the next card</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>poll</code></td><td><code>{'{ poll: 60_000 }'}</code> refreshes a value every minute for as long as the component or request that built it stands, skipping a minute its load is still running</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A confirmation goes back to how it started">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-ttl-reset"
            app="crm-04"
            title={'`reactive(false, { ttl: 2_000 })` — a flag that falls back on its own'}
            summary="A deal with a button that copies its link. Copy it, and watch the button."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealLink.abide': dealLinkSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealLink.test.ts': dealLinkTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealLink />
        </Example>

        <p><code>copied</code> has no handler and no body behind it, so its <code>ttl</code> has
            nothing to load. Instead, two seconds after a write, the value is written back to what it
            was built with (6.2). There is no <code>setTimeout</code> to clear: a second copy starts
            the two seconds over (6.11), and the timer stops with the component (6.14), so a link
            copied from a panel that then closes writes nothing afterwards.</p>

        <p>What is written back is a fresh copy of the value as it was built (6.15), so a
            <code>patch</code> on an opening list or record never changes what comes back. That
            makes the opening value plain data: one a copy cannot stand for, such as a class
            instance, is refused where the value is built (6.16).</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="After a write, reload only the entries it changed">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-refresh-stages"
            app="crm-04"
            title={'`getStage.refresh({ stage })` — reload a slice of a keyed value'}
            summary="A board of the four stages. Win a lead, and watch which stages load again."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageBoard.abide': stageBoardSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageTile.abide': stageTileSource,
                'src/pipeline/winDeal.handler.ts': winDealSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageBoard.test.ts': stageBoardTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <StageBoard />
        </Example>

        <p>Winning a lead moves one deal out of <code>lead</code> and into <code>won</code>. The
            board reloads those two entries and leaves <code>qualified</code> and
            <code>proposal</code> alone, because the argument to <code>refresh</code> is a partial
            <code>Args</code> that picks entries out of the keyed value.</p>

        <p>The triggers also come as free functions, and those take a <code>Selection</code>: the
            set of entries to act on.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>k.refresh</code>, <code>k.invalidate</code></td><td>the triggers on a keyed value, narrowed by a partial <code>Args</code>; no argument reaches every entry</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.refresh</code>, <code>rpc.invalidate</code></td><td>the same two on a handler, which is what the board calls</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>refresh</code>, <code>invalidate</code></td><td><code>refresh(getStage)</code> is <code>getStage.refresh()</code>; with no selection they reach every load in this scope</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Selection</code></td><td>a value, a keyed value, a handler, or <code>{'{ tags: [...] }'}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>tags</code></td><td><code>{'{ tags: ({ stage }) => [`stage:${stage}`] }'}</code> labels entries, so <code>{"refresh({ tags: ['stage:won'] })"}</code> reaches every value about that stage, whichever handler it came from</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`refreshes` reloads every tab showing what a write changed">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-refreshes"
            app="crm-04"
            title={"`POST(write, { refreshes: ['stage:lead'] })` — reload by tag after a write"}
            summary="The same board, with a write that says what it changed. Qualify a lead, in two tabs if you have them."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/QualifyBoard.abide': qualifyBoardSource,
                'src/pipeline/qualifyLead.handler.ts': qualifyLeadSource,
                'src/pipeline/getStage.handler.ts': getStageSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/QualifyBoard.test.ts': qualifyBoardTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <QualifyBoard />
        </Example>

        <p>The button calls <code>qualifyLead()</code> and nothing after it. The handler lists the
            tags it changes, and <code>getStage</code> tags each entry with its stage, so the
            <code>lead</code> and <code>qualified</code> entries reload and the other two do not.
            The refresh runs on the server, so every tab showing those stages reloads them, not only
            the one that clicked.</p>

        <p>It runs once the write has answered and before the answer is sent, so the caller's next
            read is already the reload. A call that is refused, by the handler or by a
            <code>middleware</code> rung, refreshes nothing, and a streamed answer refreshes when
            its last chunk is read. Where the tags depend on the call, pass a function of its
            arguments: <code>{'refreshes: ({ id }) => [`deal:${id}`]'}</code>.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>refreshes</code></td><td>the tags a write refreshes each time it answers. <code>POST</code>, <code>PUT</code>, <code>PATCH</code> and <code>DELETE</code> take it; <code>GET</code> does not, since a read changes nothing to refresh</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A search box waits for the typing to stop">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-search"
            app="crm-04"
            title={'`{ debounce }` — one search once the typing stops'}
            summary="A search over the deals. Type hopper quickly, and watch the results rather than the field."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealSearch.abide': dealSearchSource,
                'src/pipeline/searchDeals.handler.ts': searchDealsSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealSearch.test.ts': dealSearchTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealSearch />
        </Example>

        <p>The debounce is on the query, not on the search. <code>q</code> takes each keystroke and
            changes once the keys stop for 300 ms, and <code>found</code> is a derived value that calls
            <code>searchDeals</code> with it, so it runs only when <code>q</code> changes: six
            keystrokes are one request, with no debounced function to write. The results already held
            stay on screen meanwhile, and the test counts the searches to prove it.</p>

        <p>The same option on <code>found</code> would hold back its run instead (5.11). Put it on
            the value the typing writes where several things read it, so they all wait for the same
            pause.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>debounce</code></td><td>changes once the changes stop for that many ms</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>throttle</code></td><td>changes at once, then at most once per window: a live deal count that updates every second, not every message</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>identity</code></td><td>what counts as the same value: a new value equal to the held one by it wakes nobody. The default is the reference</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>structural</code></td><td><code>{'{ identity: structural }'}</code> compares by content, so a stage whose <code>{'{ count, value }'}</code> reloads unchanged does not repaint</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>transform</code></td><td><code>{'{ transform: (names) => names.toSorted() }'}</code> reshapes the results once, as they land, rather than in every reader. The function is a <code>Transformer</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`patch` changes one row of a list in place">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-patch"
            app="crm-04"
            title="`deals.patch(mutate)` — change what is held, and wake its readers"
            summary="The deal list, loaded once. Updates arrive one deal at a time; apply a few."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealFeed.abide': dealFeedSource,
                'src/pipeline/listDeals.handler.ts': listDealsSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealFeed.test.ts': dealFeedTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealFeed />
        </Example>

        <p><code>patch</code> hands you the value already held, lets you change it, and wakes what
            reads it. One update rewrites one amount: no list refetched, no row rebuilt. The test
            counts one text write and no nodes made or moved.</p>

        <p>The <code>by deal.id</code> on the loop keeps each row tied to its deal, so the row
            the update names is the only one that changes.</p>

        <p><code>patch</code> changes the array it holds and copies nothing. A path write in a
            <code>.abide</code> file, <code>{'deals[0].amount = 27_500'}</code>, also wakes the
            readers, but copies the path it writes down first. In a <code>.ts</code> file, or where
            the held value is not plain data, <code>patch</code> is the write that wakes them.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Two components far apart share one value">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-share"
            app="crm-04"
            title="`reactive.share(key, build)` — one value for a subtree"
            summary="A detail panel above a row of deals. Pick a deal, and the panel follows."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealPicker.abide': dealPickerSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealRow.abide': dealRowSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealDetail.abide': dealDetailSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealPicker.test.ts': dealPickerTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealPicker />
        </Example>

        <p><code>DealPicker</code> creates <code>selectedDeal</code>. Every row and the detail
            panel ask for the same key, find that one on the way up, and never run their own build.
            No prop runs between them.</p>

        <p>A value lives in
            the component that created it and below, so two pickers on one page each get their own.
            Where a value is one thing for the whole app, export it from a module instead.</p>

        <p><code>Shared</code> is an interface you widen with declaration merging to type each
            key. It is optional: without it, the type comes from the build function.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`watch` runs something outside the page when a value changes">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-autosave"
            app="crm-04"
            title="`watch(effect)` — save a note a beat after the typing stops"
            summary="A note on a deal, saved to the server while you type. Type a sentence quickly, and watch the saved line."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealNote.abide': dealNoteSource,
                'src/pipeline/saveNote.handler.ts': saveNoteSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealNote.test.ts': dealNoteTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealNote />
        </Example>

        <p><code>watch</code> runs its effect now, and again whenever a value it read changes.
            The effect returns a <code>Disposer</code>, which runs before the next run and when the
            component goes. Each keystroke clears the timer the one before it started, so a burst of
            typing is one save.</p>

        <p>The draft is read in the effect, not inside the timer: a read there happens after
            tracking ends and subscribes to nothing. <code>saved.peek()</code> reads the saved note
            without subscribing, so saving it does not wake the watch again.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>watch</code></td><td><code>watch(effect)</code> wakes on whatever the effect read; <code>watch(sources, effect)</code> wakes only on the values listed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.watch</code></td><td><code>watch</code> over one value, handed to the effect</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Effect</code></td><td>the function a watch runs, returning nothing or a <code>Disposer</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Disposer</code></td><td>what tears the previous run down</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.peek</code></td><td>reads what is held without subscribing</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>flushSync</code></td><td>runs every effect a write woke before it returns, for a handler that measures or scrolls the page the write left</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>isServer</code></td><td>whether the effect is running on the server, for work only a browser can do</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A watch runs on the server too, while the page renders. Guard work that needs the
            browser, such as <code>localStorage</code>, with <code>isServer()</code> rather than
            <code>typeof window</code>: a test renders the server half beside a DOM, and
            <code>isServer()</code> still answers for the side the work is on.</p>

        <p>The effect is the one place in this card where a read subscribes. Its
            <code>Disposer</code> does not, and nor does any function abide calls without rebuilding
            it:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>A read in</th><th>Subscribes</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>watch</code> effect, bare</td><td>to everything it reads</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>watch</code> effect, over sources</td><td>to the sources alone</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Transformer</code>, <code>Disposer</code>, middleware, lifecycle hooks</td><td>to nothing: none of them is rebuilt</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `store` keeps a value between visits">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-store"
            app="crm-04"
            title={'`{ store }` — where a value lives between visits'}
            summary="The quarter's target, which the whole team works to. Set a new one, then reload the page."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/TeamTarget.abide': teamTargetSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/targetStore.ts': targetStoreSource,
                'src/pipeline/getTarget.handler.ts': getTargetSource,
                'src/pipeline/setTarget.handler.ts': setTargetSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/TeamTarget.test.ts': teamTargetTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <TeamTarget />
        </Example>

        <p>A <code>Store</code> is a <code>get</code> and a <code>set</code>. The value reads the
            store when it is built and falls back to <code>500_000</code> on a miss without writing it,
            and every change is written back. The reads and writes in the markup do not change.</p>

        <p>This store goes through two handlers, so the target is one value on the server: the
            next visit opens on it, and so does everyone else's, painted right the first time. After
            a write, <code>set</code> invalidates the read it made, so the next read asks the server
            rather than this tab's copy. A setting one visitor alone sees, such as a theme, belongs
            in a cookie instead.</p>

        <p>Where the stored value can change from outside the app, such as a file another program
            edits, the store adds <code>watch</code>. It is handed a function to call when that
            happens, and returns the function that stops watching. The value reads <code>get</code>
            again, and the body does not run.</p>

        <p>Five stores come with abide, for the places a value most often lives:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Where the value lives</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>webStore</code></td><td><code>{"webStore('layout')"}</code> keeps it in this browser's <code>localStorage</code>, or <code>sessionStorage</code> with <code>{"{ area: 'session' }"}</code>, and follows a write another tab makes. On the server it is never there, so the page renders the fallback</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>sqliteStore</code></td><td><code>{"sqliteStore('crm.db', 'target')"}</code>, from <code>@abide/abide/server</code>, keeps it in a SQLite table, so a restarted server opens on it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>redisStore</code></td><td><code>{"redisStore(Bun.redis, 'target')"}</code>, from <code>@abide/abide/server</code>, keeps it in a Redis key that Redis expires with the <code>ttl</code>, so every server behind one Redis opens on it. Where Redis reports changes to its keys, it follows a write another server makes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>indexedDBStore</code></td><td><code>{"indexedDBStore('draft')"}</code> keeps it in this browser's IndexedDB, for a value too large for <code>localStorage</code>, and follows a write another tab makes. On the server it is never there, so the page renders the fallback</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>cookiesStore</code></td><td><code>{"cookiesStore('theme')"}</code> keeps it in a cookie, which the browser sends with every request, so the server renders the stored value rather than the fallback. A cookie holds 4 KB at most</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Each keeps the value under the app's name and then the key, so two apps sharing one origin
            or one Redis never read each other's. Each keeps it as JSON with the value's
            <code>ttl</code> beside it, and passes what it reads through a <code>schema</code> when
            given one: a restore skips the value's own, so an entry an older build wrote is a miss
            rather than a wrong value.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `database` keeps what a restart would lose">
        <Example
            id="crm-04-database"
            app="crm-04"
            title={"`database(name, { migrations })` — the server's own SQLite file"}
            summary="Every target the team has set, read from a file on the server. Set one, and the list still holds it after a restart."
            serverFiles={['src/pipeline/lib/crmDatabase.server.ts']}
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/TargetLog.abide': targetLogSource,
                'src/pipeline/listTargets.handler.ts': listTargetsSource,
                'src/pipeline/setTarget.handler.ts': setTargetSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/TargetLog.test.ts': targetLogTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <TargetLog />
        </Example>

        <p><code>database</code>, from <code>@abide/abide/server</code>, hands back a function. Its
            first call opens <code>crm.sqlite</code> in <code>APP_DATA_DIR</code>, and every call
            after hands back the same <code>bun:sqlite</code> <code>Database</code>, so a handler
            writes plain SQL against it. Each chapter's app has its own <code>APP_DATA_DIR</code>,
            named for the app, so two apps never share the file.</p>

        <p>Each migration runs once, in order, and the file remembers how many it has run. A new
            column is one more string at the end of the list, never an edit to one already there.
            A migration can be a function too, for rows a first boot seeds. If one throws, none of
            that boot's migrations are kept, and a file a newer build migrated is refused rather
            than opened.</p>

        <p>Under <code>bun test</code> each database is an empty one in memory, so a test never
            writes the file the app serves. The app closes the file as it stops. A
            <code>sqliteStore</code> takes what the function hands back, so a store and the app's
            own tables can share one file.</p>
    </Section>
    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/guide/make-it-live')}>Make it live</a> — every open tab sees a
                change as it happens</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
